---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The RawBars reference — a focused, honest core-surface page (design debate
// consensus). RawBars is the desugared core surface: no surface sugar, every value an
// explicit helper application. Prose illustrates; runnable examples come from the
// single gate-validated source ../rawbars.mjs. One flagship example shows the
// live compiled-JS pane (RawBars compiles; MinBars doesn't). The deep-dive links
// the spec/ADR rather than forking them, grounded in the page's own live
// artifacts (the diptych = desugar; the compile pane = compile).
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as ex } from "../rawbars.mjs";

const SPEC_BASE = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SPEC_BASE || "/flatbars/spec/";

const groups = [
  {
    label: "RawBars",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What RawBars is" },
      { href: "#output", label: "Output & escaping" },
      { href: "#application", label: "Application & parens" },
      { href: "#lookup", label: "Reading data" },
      { href: "#blocks", label: "Blocks & scope" },
      { href: "#compile", label: "Compiles to JS" },
      { href: "#conditionals", label: "Conditionals" },
      { href: "#comments", label: "Comments" },
      { href: "#architecture", label: "Under the hood" },
      { href: "#helpers", label: "Helpers" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "New here?",
    items: [{ href: "/start", label: "Getting started" }],
  },
  {
    label: "Other surfaces",
    items: [
      { href: "/minbars", label: "MinBars — Mustache" },
      { href: "/fullbars", label: "FullBars — Handlebars" },
      { href: "/maxbars", label: "MaxBars — operators" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=rawbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];

// Inline tag references — kept in a helper so Astro never parses the {{ }}.
const t = (s) => s;
---

<Reference
  title="RawBars — the desugared core surface"
  subtitle="RawBars is the desugared core surface of FlatBars: the core skeleton syntax exposed as a runnable dialect, with no surface sugar — every value is an explicit helper application. You rarely hand-write it — it's the desugared form the sugar-rich surfaces (Mustache, Handlebars) lower to. This reference shows the bare core syntax running live, and compiling to JS."
  groups={groups}
  spec="concepts"
  dialect="rawbars"
>
  <style>
    .terms { margin: 1.3rem 0; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--bg-1); }
    .terms .terms-h { margin: 0 0 0.55rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--fg-secondary); font-size: 0.95rem; }
    .terms dl { margin: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 0.35rem 1rem; }
    .terms dt { font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent-2); }
    .terms dd { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-muted); }
    .terms dt .alt { font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-faint); font-size: 0.82em; }
    @media (max-width: 560px) { .terms dl { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .terms dd { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; } }
  </style>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What RawBars is</h2>
    <p>
      RawBars renders the <b>core skeleton syntax</b> directly — no dotted paths,
      no auto-escaping, no <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code> interpolation. There is one
      rule: <b>everything is a helper application</b>. <code>{t("{{{city}}}")}</code>
      doesn't read a variable named <code>city</code> — it <em>calls</em> the
      helper <code>city</code>. To read a field you call <code>lookup</code>; to
      escape you call <code>escapeHtml</code>. Nothing is implicit.
    </p>
    <p>
      That makes RawBars <b>verbose on purpose</b>. The verbosity is the feature:
      nothing is hidden, so it's the honest substrate to target when you build a
      friendlier surface. The dialect ladder runs <b>RawBars → FullBars → MaxBars</b>,
      each adding surface sugar over the same core; end users reach for
      <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a> or the flagship <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a>,
      which buy back the brevity. They all share
      one engine, prelude, and compiler — they differ only in surface syntax.
    </p>

    <aside class="terms">
      <p class="terms-h">Four words this site uses precisely:</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Core</dt>
        <dd>the <b>meaning-free parser</b> (the <code>flatbars</code> package). It gives a tag
          <em>structure</em>, never meaning. RawBars is the core's bare surface.</dd>
        <dt>Surface</dt>
        <dd>the <b>syntax you write</b>. FlatBars has four: RawBars ⊂ FullBars ⊂ MaxBars form the
          <em>dialect ladder</em> (each adds sugar over the same core); MinBars is a
          Mustache-compatible peer.</dd>
        <dt>Engine</dt>
        <dd>the <b>runtime that assigns meaning</b> — operations, scope, truthiness. The FlatBars
          dialects share one.</dd>
        <dt>Operation <span class="alt">(host API: helper)</span></dt>
        <dd>the named thing a tag <b>applies</b>; there are no variables, so even
          <code>{t("{{{city}}}")}</code> applies the operation <code>city</code>. The JS host API
          keeps the word <em>helper</em> (<code>registerHelper</code>).</dd>
      </dl>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Output & escaping ───────────────────── -->
  <section id="output">
    <h2>Output &amp; escaping — sugar → core</h2>
    <p>
      RawBars has one inline output form, the triple-stash <code>{t("{{{ … }}}")}</code>,
      and it is <b>never auto-escaped</b>. Here is what FullBars's friendly
      <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> actually <em>is</em> — the same render, written
      out explicitly:
    </p>
    <p class="diptych-cap"><b>FullBars</b> (sugar):</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine={ex.sugar.engine} template={ex.sugar.template} data={ex.sugar.data} />
    <p class="diptych-cap"><b>RawBars</b> (the same thing, explicit):</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.core.template} data={ex.core.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      Same output, <code>Ada &amp;lt;core&amp;gt;</code>. FullBars' <code>{t("{{x}}")}</code>
      desugars to exactly <code>{t("{{{escapeHtml (lookup this \"x\")}}}")}</code>.
      The sugar is convenience; the core is the contract.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Application & parens ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="application">
    <h2>Application is juxtaposition; parens group</h2>
    <p>
      <code>head arg1 arg2</code> applies <code>head</code> to its arguments by
      juxtaposition. Parentheses do <b>not</b> mean "call" — they <em>group</em> a
      multi-token application so it fits one argument slot.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.application.template} data={ex.application.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      <code>{t("uppercase (lookup this \"name\")")}</code> applies <code>uppercase</code>
      to <b>one</b> argument — the parenthesised group. Drop the parens —
      <code>{t("uppercase lookup this \"name\"")}</code> — and you'd be applying
      <code>uppercase</code> to <em>three</em> arguments. That's the one parse rule
      to internalise.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── lookup ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="lookup">
    <h2>Reading data — explicitly</h2>
    <p>
      RawBars has no dot paths. You index data with <code>lookup</code>:
      <code>{t("(lookup this \"k\")")}</code> reads a field of the current context;
      add more keys to descend. <code>this</code> is itself a (nullary) helper —
      the current context.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.lookup.template} data={ex.lookup.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Blocks & scope ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="blocks">
    <h2>Blocks install scope</h2>
    <p>
      A block <code>{t("{{#helper args}} … {{/helper}}")}</code> hands its body
      to a block helper. <code>each</code> iterates and, inside the body, installs
      scoped helpers — <code>this</code> (the element), <code>index</code>,
      <code>key</code>, <code>first</code>, <code>last</code> — that the block
      removes again on exit.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.each.template} data={ex.each.data} compile />

    <!-- ───── Compiles to JS (flagship, lives on the each card above) ───── -->
    <h3 id="compile">…and it compiles to JS</h3>
    <p>
      The <b>compiled JS</b> pane above is live: the very template the interpreter
      runs also compiles to a standalone ES module (it runs against
      <code>flatbars-runtime.mjs</code>). This is RawBars' edge over MinBars, which
      is render-only. The compiler is held to <b>byte-identical output with the
      interpreter</b> — the repo asserts it on every build (<code>npm run
      test:compile</code>), so the pane is the same bytes you see under
      <code>output</code>, just produced ahead of time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Conditionals ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conditionals">
    <h2>Conditionals — and no keywords</h2>
    <p>
      <code>if</code> is a block helper, not syntax. And <code>else</code> is not a
      keyword: it's a bare <b>separator</b> <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code> that the
      core keeps as a meaningless marker and the <code>if</code> helper splits its
      body on. <code>else</code>, <code>elif</code>, <code>case</code> are all the
      same shape to the parser.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.cond.template} data={ex.cond.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Comments ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="comments">
    <h2>Comments</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{! … }}")}</code> is dropped by the lexer and never reaches the
      output. (A comment can also carry a header directive, e.g.
      <code>{t("{{! @trim: none }}")}</code> — but that's a separate topic.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.comment.template} data={ex.comment.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Under the hood ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="architecture">
    <h2>Under the hood — one core, four pluggable rules</h2>
    <p>
      RawBars is the bare expression of the design the whole project rests on
      (<a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0001-structural-parser-and-walker/`}>ADR-0001</a>,
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}concepts/`}>concepts</a>): a <b>meaning-free
      parser</b> builds a structural tree, and a pluggable rule supplies the
      meaning — <i>"don't call us, we call you."</i> The same idea appears four
      times, and you've just seen two of them run:
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>IoC instance</th><th>What plugs in</th><th>On this page</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>interpret</td><td>the engine (helper meanings)</td><td>every example's <code>output</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>desugar</td><td>a surface's rewrite rules</td><td>the sugar → core diptych above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>compile</td><td>a per-dialect emitter</td><td>the <code>compiled JS</code> pane</td></tr>
        <tr><td>validate</td><td>a schema</td><td>the linter (off-page)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A dialect is just a different <b>surface</b> swapped in at one seam: the
      parser tokenises a tag's interior with a shared tokeniser and hands it to a
      dialect grammar (<code>parseExpr</code> / <code>parseHead</code>). RawBars
      uses the plain core grammar; MaxBars plugs in infix operators and pipes.
      Which tag <em>shapes</em> are legal is also dialect-gated — RawBars sets
      <code>extras = false</code>, so it rejects the Handlebars-only shapes
      (<code>{t("{{^ }}")}</code> inverse, <code>{t("{{& }}")}</code> unescaped,
      <code>{t("{{{{ }}}}")}</code> raw blocks). That seam is how
      <a href="/minbars">MinBars</a> and <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a> are
      built on the same core.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Helpers ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="helpers">
    <h2>Helpers</h2>
    <p>
      RawBars exposes the whole reference prelude (~60 helpers). The handful these
      examples use:
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Helper</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="code">this</span></td><td>the current context (nullary)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">lookup</span></td><td><span class="code">{t("(lookup obj k1 k2 …)")}</span> — index by key/position</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">escapeHtml</span></td><td>HTML-escape a value</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">uppercase</span></td><td>upper-case a string</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">each</span></td><td>block: iterate an array/object, installing scope</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">if</span> / <span class="code">unless</span></td><td>block: conditional; split on the <span class="code">{t("{{else}}")}</span> separator</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="code">index</span></td><td>scoped: 0-based position inside <span class="code">each</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The full list — comparisons, arithmetic, string/array helpers, the scoped
      loop variables — is the generated
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}engine/prelude/`}>helper catalog</a>. Every one is
      available identically in FullBars and MaxBars.
    </p>
    <p>
      One scoped helper has no surface sugar: <code>{t("{{{yield}}}")}</code> renders
      the caller's body inside a block partial
      (<code>{t("{{#partial \"name\" ctx}} … {{/partial}}")}</code>). It is RawBars'
      spelling of Handlebars' <code>{t("{{> @partial-block}}")}</code> — MaxBars
      writes the bare <code>{t("{{yield}}")}</code>, and all three name the one
      scoped <code>partial-block</code> operation. A partial can be defined inline with
      <code>{t("{{#inline \"name\"}} … {{/inline}}")}</code> (hoisted into the registry,
      the same shared engine step FullBars and MaxBars run) — so partials/inline/yield
      are core capability, not surface sugar; RawBars differs from the others only in
      syntax.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="rawbars" template={ex.yield.template} data={ex.yield.data} />

    <h3>Where to go next</h3>
    <p>
      You rarely write RawBars by hand — it's the desugared core surface you target. For everyday
      templating, climb the ladder: <a href="/minbars">MinBars</a> is the
      logic-less on-ramp, <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a> adds Handlebars-style blocks
      and helpers, and <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> adds infix operators and pipes —
      all desugaring to the core you just saw. To compute a value before rendering, see
      <a href="/transform">Data shaping</a>. Or open the bare core in the
      <a href="/lab/index.html?engine=rawbars">Lab</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

</Reference>
